  "worked_solution": "The local truncation error (LTE) is the error made in a *single step* of a numerical method, when the step begins from an *exact value*. The LTE of a numerical method at step $n$ from $x$ with exact value ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)$ to ${x}_{\\text{new}}=x+Δx$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{n} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-{y}_{\\text{new}} \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}})-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)+Δy).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that the exact solution is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(x)={x}^{2}+4x.$ So, the $y$-value at the start of the step $x=1$ is ${y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)=5.$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-increment given by the numerical method.\n\nFirst, the slope at the start of the step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1) & =2(1)+4 \\\\  & =2+4 \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the increment at this step is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}+{y}_{\\text{exact}}^{′}(1))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1+6)\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & ≈0.4667.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the exact value at ${x}_{\\text{new}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{\\text{exact}}({x}_{\\text{new}}) & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(x+Δx) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1+0.2) \\\\  & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2) \\\\  & =(1.2)^{2}+4(1.2) \\\\  & =6.24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the LTE for the step starting at $x=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{τ}_{6} & ={y}_{\\text{exact}}(1.2)-({y}_{\\text{exact}}(1)+Δy) \\\\  & =6.24-(5+0.4667) \\\\  & ≈0.773\\end{aligned}\n$$",
